## Installation

### Step 1 — Install Python 3.12

1. Download Python **3.12.10** from <https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-31210/> (scroll down to **Windows installer (64-bit)**).
2. Run the installer. On the first screen, **check the box "Add python.exe to PATH"** before clicking *Install Now*. This is required — it also sets up the file association that lets you double-click the app later.
3. Open a **new** Command Prompt (`cmd.exe`) and confirm it worked:
   ```bat
   python --version
   python -m pip install --upgrade pip
   ```
   You should see `Python 3.12.10`.

> Stick with 3.12. Newer Python versions (3.13+) don't have matching PyTorch/NeMo wheels here.

### Step 2 — Install Git

You need Git both to clone the repo and to install NeMo (pip pulls NeMo straight from GitHub).

- Download and install **Git for Windows** from <https://git-scm.com/download/win> (the defaults are fine).
- *Alternative:* if you'd rather not install Git, you can download the repo as a ZIP from the green **Code → Download ZIP** button on GitHub and extract it — but Git is still needed for the NeMo install in Step 5.

### Step 3 — Get the code

```sh
git clone https://github.com/kscrudders/Audio2Text.git
```

Note where it lands — you'll reuse this path. These instructions assume:

```
C:\Users\<you>\Audio2Text
```

### Step 4 — Create the virtual environment *inside the project folder*

> **Important:** Create the environment **in the project root itself**, not in a separate `.venv` folder. Audio2Text auto-detects and re-launches itself using `Scripts\pythonw.exe` located next to the main script. If the environment lives anywhere else, that auto-relaunch won't find it and the app won't start.

```bat
cd C:\Users\<you>\Audio2Text
python -m venv .
```

This adds `Scripts\`, `Lib\`, and `pyvenv.cfg` alongside the source files. Now activate it (you only need to do this for the install steps below):

```bat
C:\Users\<you>\Audio2Text\Scripts\activate
```

Your prompt should now be prefixed with the environment name. (Use **Command Prompt**, not PowerShell — PowerShell's execution policy often blocks the activate script.

### Step 5 — Install the Python packages

With the environment activated:

```bat
python -m pip install --upgrade pip setuptools

:: --- GUI + audio ---
pip install pyaudio numpy webrtcvad-wheels
pip install customtkinter packaging

:: --- NeMo and its dependencies (needs Git from Step 2) ---
pip install sacrebleu
pip install fsspec==2024.12.0
pip install "nemo_toolkit[asr] @ git+https://github.com/NVIDIA/NeMo.git"
pip install hf_xet
```

### Step 6 — Install PyTorch for your GPU (do this LAST)

Install PyTorch **after** NeMo, so it overrides the generic build NeMo pulls in. **Pick exactly one** command for your GPU generation — the CUDA build must match your card, or the model can crash while loading.

**RTX 50-series (Blackwell):** PyTorch nightly, CUDA 12.8
```bat
pip install --pre torch torchvision torchaudio --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/nightly/cu128
```

**RTX 30 / 40-series:** PyTorch stable, CUDA 12.4
```bat
pip install torch torchvision torchaudio --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu124
```
> On 30-series cards the nightly cu128 build has been observed to crash on model load — use the stable cu124 build above.

Verify CUDA is visible:
```bat
python -c "import torch; print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available())"
```
The second value should be `True`.

### Step 7 — Add FFmpeg

FFmpeg is required to load compressed audio (MP3, M4A, FLAC, OGG). Recording to WAV works without it, but the **Load Audio** button needs it for non-WAV files.

1. Go to <https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/> and download **`ffmpeg-git-full.7z`**. (You'll need [7-Zip](https://www.7-zip.org/) to extract a `.7z` archive.) The official source page is also at <https://www.ffmpeg.org/download.html#build-windows>.
2. Inside the extracted folder, open `bin\`. Copy **`ffmpeg.exe`** **and** **`ffprobe.exe`** into the application directory — the same folder as the main script:
   ```
   C:\Users\<you>\Audio2Text\ffmpeg.exe
   C:\Users\<you>\Audio2Text\ffprobe.exe
   ```
   The app looks for `ffmpeg.exe` there (and uses `ffprobe.exe` if present).

### Step 8 — Run it

You do **not** need to activate the environment to run the app — the script relaunches itself using the environment you created in Step 4.

- **Double-click** the main script (`Audio2Text.pyw`) in `C:\Users\<you>\Audio2Text`.
- Or from an activated Command Prompt: `python Audio2Text.pyw`

A small splash appears immediately, then the main window opens showing **"Loading AI engine…"** while PyTorch/NeMo load in the background. When that finishes you'll be asked to pick a model. The first time you choose a model it downloads from Hugging Face, so give it a few minutes.

